<section id="s-cascade" data-code="cascade">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Cascade Problem</div>
<h2>Why One Wrong Token Can Cost You Sixteen</h2>
<p>Speculative decoding works by drafting a block of γ tokens at once, then verifying them all in a single target-model forward pass. The speedup comes from parallelizing that verification: instead of γ sequential target calls, you do one. But there's a nasty catch built into the acceptance protocol.</p>
<p>The verification proceeds left-to-right. If token position k fails (the target model disagrees with the draft), every position k+1 through γ is automatically rejected—regardless of whether those later tokens were correct. This <strong>cascade rejection</strong> is the fundamental bottleneck. A single early error wastes the entire draft budget.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Token cascade — where does the first error occur?</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Click a position to see the rejection cascade</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:220px">
    <div class="btn-group" id="cascade-btns">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" data-pos="0">Error at 1</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-pos="3">Error at 4</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-pos="7">Error at 8</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" data-pos="15">No error (16)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="cascade-canvas" style="width:100%;height:140px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="cascade-stat" style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2);margin-top:.75rem;text-align:center"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The DFlash paper reports an average acceptance length τ ≈ 6.5 tokens on Qwen3-8B at temperature 0. That means the average draft block (γ = 16) contributes only 6–7 accepted tokens per round. Improving τ is the single lever that raises wall-clock speedup.</p>
<p>Three forces drive τ down: (1) poor draft quality in early positions—those positions receive less context, (2) error cascades magnifying individual mistakes, and (3) distribution mismatch—the draft model's token probabilities don't tightly match the target's. LLaDA 2.0 and 2.1 contain tools that directly address all three.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>Speedup formula:</strong> The per-token latency L = (T<sub>draft</sub> + T<sub>verify</sub>) / τ. Doubling τ roughly halves latency. All five techniques below aim at τ—and are orthogonal, so gains compound.</p>
</div>
</section>

