<section id="s-dflash" data-code="dflash">
<div class="section-no">02 — DFlash Recap</div>
<h2>Parallel Drafting with a Diffusion Adapter</h2>
<p>DFlash's key insight: the target LLM's internal hidden states implicitly encode predictions about future tokens. Rather than forcing a tiny draft model to reason from scratch, DFlash extracts those hidden states and injects them directly into the draft model's KV caches—making the drafter a lightweight adapter rather than an independent reasoner.</p>

<p>At inference, the system works in two phases. During prefill the target model processes the prompt and generates the first output token; DFlash captures 5 uniformly sampled hidden layers and fuses them via a projection into a single <em>context feature</em>. In the drafting phase, this context feature is stored as persistent KV entries in all 5 layers of the draft model. The draft model then denoises a block of 16 <code>&lt;mask&gt;</code> tokens <strong>in a single forward pass</strong>—all positions in parallel—and the result goes to the target LLM for verification.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">DFlash inference architecture</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">KV injection ensures deep conditioning throughout the draft model</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg id="arch-svg" viewBox="0 0 680 230" style="width:100%;height:auto;overflow:visible" role="img" aria-label="DFlash architecture diagram"></svg>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Three training choices are especially important for understanding where improvement is possible. First, <strong>shared embedding &amp; LM head</strong>: the draft model reuses the target model's token embeddings and output projection (both frozen), so it operates in the same representation space. Second, <strong>random anchor sampling</strong>: during training, a random clean token within each block is chosen as the anchor, exposing the model to a diverse range of target context features. Third, <strong>exponential loss weighting</strong>: earlier positions within a block get higher loss weight (w<sub>k</sub> = exp(−(k−1)/γ)), acknowledging the cascade structure.</p>

