<p>The question now is: DFlash's draft model is fundamentally a small diffusion LM. LLaDA 2.0 and 2.1 each developed new training techniques for exactly this class of model. Can those techniques be transplanted?</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-llada20" data-code="llada20">
<div class="section-no">03 — LLaDA 2.0</div>
<h2>CAP Training and WSD Initialization</h2>
<p>LLaDA 2.0 is a 100B-parameter block-diffusion model built by converting a pre-trained autoregressive model rather than training from scratch. Two of its contributions are directly portable to DFlash's draft model.</p>

<h3>Technique 1 — CAP: Confidence-Aware Parallel Training</h3>
<p>Standard masked diffusion training treats every predicted token equally: minimize cross-entropy at all masked positions. But for speculative decoding, prediction confidence matters more than just accuracy. The verifier's acceptance decision is probabilistic—a token is accepted with probability p<sub>target</sub>(x) / p<sub>draft</sub>(x). If the draft model predicts "Paris" with probability 0.42 and the target model predicts "Paris" with probability 0.91, the acceptance probability is 0.42/0.91 ≈ 0.46. But if CAP training sharpens the draft model to predict "Paris" with probability 0.85, the same token now accepts with probability 0.85/0.91 ≈ 0.93.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Token confidence distribution — CE vs CAP training</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Click to toggle between training regimes</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:240px">
    <div class="btn-group">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="conf-ce-btn">Standard CE</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="conf-cap-btn">+ CAP Training</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="conf-canvas" style="width:100%;height:170px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="conf-stat" style="font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2);margin-top:.75rem;text-align:center"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>CAP adds an auxiliary confidence loss that minimizes entropy specifically for <em>correctly predicted</em> tokens:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">CAP Training Loss</div>
  ℒ = ℒ<sub>CE</sub>(θ) + <span class="math-var">λ</span> · ℒ<sub>conf</sub>(θ)<br>
  ℒ<sub>conf</sub>(θ) = −𝔼[Σ<sub>i: correct</sub> Σ<sub>v</sub> p<sub>θ</sub>(v) log p<sub>θ</sub>(v)] <span class="math-comment">  ← entropy on correct-token positions only</span>
</div>

<p>In LLaDA 2.0's results, adding CAP to SFT pushed throughput from 3.08 tokens-per-forward (TPF) to 5.93 TPF in Speed Mode—a 1.9× gain—without sacrificing task accuracy. For DFlash, the mechanism would differ slightly (DFlash uses single-step diffusion, not iterative), but the same principle holds: sharper distributions align better with the target, raising the acceptance probability at each position.</p>

<h3>Technique 2 — WSD: Warmup-Stable-Decay Initialization</h3>
<p>DFlash trains its 5-layer diffusion model from random initialization. LLaDA 2.0 showed that a far better starting point is an <em>existing autoregressive model</em>, converted to diffusion via a 3-phase schedule:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Warmup</strong>: Block size grows 1 → 4 → 32 → 64 → full-sequence. At block size 1, the model is equivalent to AR. Gradual expansion lets the model keep its AR knowledge while learning diffusion dynamics.</li>
  <li><strong>Stable</strong>: Full masked diffusion training at fixed block size. The model learns global bidirectional context.</li>
  <li><strong>Decay</strong>: Block size shrinks back to 32. This "distills" the global knowledge into a compact block representation suited for KV-cache–friendly inference.</li>
</ul>

<p>For DFlash, this means starting from a small pre-trained AR model—say, a 350M parameter Qwen3-mini—and converting it via WSD before fine-tuning with DFlash's speculative decoding objective. The initialized model would understand language far better than a randomly initialized 5-layer network, potentially pushing τ from ~6.5 toward 8+ in the early training phase.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-t2t" data-code="t2t">
<div class="section-no">04 — LLaDA 2.1</div>
<h2>Token-to-Token Editing as Draft Self-Correction</h2>
<p>LLaDA 2.1 introduces a fundamental extension to the block-diffusion decoding loop: Token-to-Token (T2T) editing. In standard masked diffusion (M2T, Mask-to-Token), the model can only convert a <code>&lt;mask&gt;</code> to a real token. T2T adds a second operation: convert a real token to a <em>different</em> real token. The model can revisit and revise its previous outputs.</p>

<p>Decoding at each step is governed by two thresholds:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">LLaDA 2.1 Dual-Threshold Decoding</div>
  Unmask set: Γ<sub>t</sub> = &#123; i | x<sub>t</sub><sup>i</sup> = [MASK] and p(v | x<sub>t</sub>) &gt; τ<sub>mask</sub> &#125;<br>
  Edit set:   Δ<sub>t</sub> = &#123; i | x<sub>t</sub><sup>i</sup> ≠ v and p(v | x<sub>t</sub>) &gt; τ<sub>edit</sub> &#125;<br>
  Update: x<sub>t−1</sub><sup>i</sup> = v  if  i ∈ Γ<sub>t</sub> ∪ Δ<sub>t</sub>, else x<sub>t</sub><sup>i</sup>
</div>

<p>In <strong>Speed (S) Mode</strong>, τ<sub>mask</sub> is lowered aggressively—many tokens get unmasked even with moderate confidence. Those fast-but-uncertain tokens become candidates for T2T correction in subsequent steps. In <strong>Quality (Q) Mode</strong>, τ<sub>mask</sub> stays high (conservative unmasking) but T2T still catches the rare errors that slip through.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">T2T self-correction in DFlash context — a 3-step inference loop</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Navigate the proposed draft-and-edit pipeline</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="t2t-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" id="t2t-step-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Parallel M2T Draft</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The 5-layer draft model denoises all 16 mask tokens in one forward pass. Output: 16 candidate tokens with associated confidences. Example result: <strong>["The", "capital", "of", "Frnace", "is", "Paris", …]</strong> — position 4 misspelled.</div>
          <div class="step-code">draft = diffusion_forward(context_features, [MASK]×16)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="t2t-step-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">T2T Editing Pass</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A second forward pass identifies tokens where the model's confidence in a <em>different</em> token exceeds τ<sub>edit</sub>. Position 4 ("Frnace") gets edited to "France". Output: <strong>["The", "capital", "of", "France", "is", "Paris", …]</strong> — all correct.</div>
          <div class="step-code">refined = t2t_edit(draft, tau_edit=0.85)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="t2t-step-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target Verification</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The refined block is submitted to the target LLM. Left-to-right token-by-token acceptance: all 16 tokens pass. <strong>τ = 16</strong> for this round vs τ ≈ 4 without the editing pass (cascade stops at position 4).</div>
          <div class="step-code">accepted = target_verify(refined, target_logits)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="t2t-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="t2t-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 3</span>
      <button id="t2t-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The cost of the T2T pass is one additional forward pass through the draft model (not the target model). Because the draft model is tiny (5 layers, shared embeddings), this is negligible compared to the target verification cost. LLaDA 2.1 trains for T2T by creating a parallel data augmentation stream: for a clean sequence, randomly perturb some tokens, then train the model to recover the original. This teaches the model to recognize and correct its own likely error patterns.</p>

<p>For DFlash integration, the most natural approach is adding a T2T loss term alongside DFlash's existing CE loss, using the target model's generated responses as both the masking target (standard DFlash) and the editing supervision (T2T stream).</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-ebpo" data-code="ebpo">
<div class="section-no">05 — LLaDA 2.1: EBPO</div>
<h2>Training Directly for Acceptance Rate</h2>
<p>Here's the uncomfortable truth about DFlash's training objective: it minimizes per-token cross-entropy against the target model's predictions, but the thing that actually matters for speedup is the <em>acceptance rate</em> in speculative decoding. Cross-entropy is a proxy. EBPO, introduced in LLaDA 2.1, provides a principled way to replace that proxy with the real thing.</p>

<p>EBPO (ELBO-Based Block-level Policy Optimization) adapts PPO-style RL to the diffusion LM setting. The key challenge for RL in diffusion models is that the standard token-level log-probability is intractable to compute exactly. LLaDA 2.1 solves this by using the block-diffusion ELBO as a differentiable proxy:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">EBPO Objective</div>
  J<sub>EBPO</sub>(θ) = 𝔼<sub>y~π<sub>old</sub></sub>[ min( ρ(y|x) · Â , clip(ρ(y|x), 1−ε, 1+ε) · Â ) ]<br><br>
  log ρ(y|x) ≈ Σ<sub>n</sub> w<sub>n</sub> Σ<sub>b</sub> [ log p<sub>θ</sub>(y<sup>b</sup>|z<sub>n</sub>, x) − log p<sub>θ<sub>old</sub></sub>(y<sup>b</sup>|z<sub>n</sub>, x) ]<br>
  <span class="math-comment">  where z<sub>n</sub> = y<sub>t<sub>n</sub></sub> ⊕ y<sub>0</sub> is a composite diffusion-noised input at timestep t<sub>n</sub></span>
</div>

<p>For DFlash, the reward signal is beautifully simple: after each speculative decoding round, the verifier tells you exactly which tokens were accepted. The reward for position k is 1 if token k was accepted, 0 otherwise. This is a direct, lossless signal—there's no need for a proxy reward model or human preference labels.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Acceptance length τ over training — CE loss vs EBPO fine-tuning</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Projected curves based on EBPO improvements seen in LLaDA 2.1</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:210px">
    <canvas id="ebpo-chart" style="width:100%;height:180px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>LLaDA 2.1's EBPO achieved a +1.1 point average score improvement across 33 benchmarks with matched compute. In DFlash's case, the improvement might be larger because the reward signal is more tightly aligned: instead of a general quality reward, EBPO would directly optimize the acceptance probability chain τ = 𝔼[Σ<sub>k</sub> 𝟙(accepted<sub>k</sub>)].</p>

<p>One important detail: EBPO requires computing log-probability ratios across diffusion timesteps in parallel (vectorized likelihood estimation). LLaDA 2.1's infrastructure (custom SGLang rollout engine) handles this at scale. For DFlash, a simpler rollout can be used—run the draft model, collect the verifier's accept/reject decisions as rewards, and update via the EBPO surrogate objective. The infrastructure complexity is lower because DFlash's draft model uses a single denoising step (no multi-timestep rollout needed for drafting).</p>

<h3>MBE: Retroactive Multi-Block Correction</h3>
<p>LLaDA 2.1 also introduces Multiple Block Editing (MBE), which allows the model to revise tokens in <em>previously generated blocks</em> based on content in the current block. For DFlash, an analogous extension would let the draft model condition on later-block context to refine earlier-block drafts before the cascade verification. LLaDA 2.1's ablation shows MBE adds +2.0 pp on average at only −11.7% TPF cost—strong evidence that retroactive correction is worth its compute price.</p>
</section>

