<h3>Open Research Questions</h3>
<p>Several non-trivial questions remain before DFlash++ becomes a reality:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>T2T latency budget</strong>: The second draft-model forward pass for T2T editing costs one additional small-model inference. At 5 layers and shared embeddings, this is sub-millisecond on modern GPUs—but the interaction with DFlash's Flex Attention sparse mask (which operates across multiple draft blocks simultaneously) needs careful implementation.</li>
  <li><strong>EBPO rollout stability</strong>: EBPO requires online rollouts during training. For DFlash, this means running the full target model (frozen) during draft model fine-tuning. LLaDA 2.1 handled this with a custom SGLang engine; DFlash would need a similar infrastructure piece.</li>
  <li><strong>WSD block size schedule</strong>: LLaDA 2.0 sweeps block sizes from 1 to 4096. DFlash's maximum block size is 16. Does the WSD benefit transfer at smaller scales, or does the full-sequence MDM phase (block size = 4096) provide crucial long-range structure that a size-16 drafter never uses?</li>
  <li><strong>Distribution shift from T2T mixing</strong>: DFlash's training distribution consists of clean prompt + clean target response (with random masking). T2T adds perturbed (noisy) input sequences. If the perturbation distribution doesn't match the drafter's actual error distribution, the editing pass might learn to "correct" tokens that aren't actually wrong.</li>
</ul>
</section>

